GAME CONCEPT
Loving Away is a multiplayer arena shooter where 2-4 players compete in fast-paced combat.
The game features a cute, cozy art style inspired by Animal Party, with physics-based movement
that gives characters a satisfying "weight" and momentum feel.
Players control characters from a top-down perspective and use a charging mechanic to launch
projectiles at opponents. The longer you hold the shoot button, the farther your projectile flies.
When you shoot, your character gets pushed backward slightly. When hit by a projectile, players
get knocked back significantly, potentially pushing them toward the arena boundaries where
they can be eliminated.
The key feature is the "heavy" character movement - players have momentum and inertia, so
you can't instantly change direction. This creates strategic depth as players must predict where
opponents will slide to and time their shots carefully.
GAMEPLAY FEATURES
• Charge-and-shoot mechanic: hold the button longer to shoot farther with a higher arc
• Physics-based movement with momentum, sliding, and knockback effects
• Top-down camera view (angled 20-60 degrees)
• Arena boundaries that eliminate players who go too far outside
• Shadow effects showing projectile height for better aiming
• Cute visual style with projectiles that look like hearts, thumbs up, or other friendly emojis
NETWORKING RELATED CHARACTERISTICS
This game is designed specifically to showcase network programming challenges. The real-time
combat and physics-based movement mean that any network issues will be immediately
obvious to players:
Latency (lag): Delayed hit detection will make projectiles miss moving targets. The charging
mechanic requires precise timing, so input delay will be very frustrating. Knockback effects
happening late will confuse players about what hit them.
Jitter (unstable connection): Characters will move in jerky, unpredictable ways. Projectile arcs
will look wrong. The momentum-based movement will feel broken when updates arrive
inconsistently.
Packet Loss: Missing updates will cause characters to suddenly teleport. Projectiles might
disappear or appear out of nowhere. Hits won't register properly, making the game unfair.
The game meets the course requirement that network problems must clearly impair gameplay.
Unlike turn-based games where lag is less noticeable, this real-time competitive game will feel
terrible with poor network conditions.
TECHNICAL APPROACH
Unity Version: 6.2.6f2 (Latest Recommended Support Version)
The game will use a client-server architecture where one player hosts the game and acts as the
server. The server is the authority for all game state - it decides where players are, whether
projectiles hit, and how knockback is applied.
For the physics-based movement, I'll use a simplified approach instead of Unity's complex
physics engine. Characters will move using basic math (velocity, acceleration, drag) which is
easier to synchronize over the network and gives consistent results. This approach still creates
the "heavy" feel of momentum and sliding, but without the networking headaches of a full
physics simulation.
Projectiles follow a simple curved path calculated from the charge amount. The server
determines the trajectory and clients just need to know the start point, end point, and how high
it goes to draw the same arc.
